Ports upstream PR vllm-project/vllm#66026 (open) to this pin.

_gather_draft_sampled_kernel in the V2 runner's rejection sampler is a plain @triton.jit kernel that takes
num_logits, which changes with the batch. Triton specializes the integer, and the rejection-sampler warm-up compiles
one request's shape, therefore the first concurrent requests compile the kernel again inside serving. On an RTX 3090
with the single-user launcher's defaults (MTP k=4, max_num_seqs 8) and a cold cache, two boots each logged it as a
JIT compilation during inference, 6.0 s and 11.6 s into an 8-stream burst, and the streams running at that moment
waited 178 ms between tokens where the worst gap otherwise was about 100 ms. 0.32.0 compiles it the same way
(docs/vllm-0.33.md, "First-request JIT"). Stock vLLM (a 0.33.0rc1 nightly, Qwen3-0.6B, ngram k=4) shows the same
compile and a 68-80 ms stall.

The PR marks num_logits do_not_specialize, as #64137 did for the logits-stats kernels, so one compiled kernel serves
every batch. With it, two more boots under the same load compiled nothing during serving.

Applied unchanged: the PR's one-line change applies to 0.33.0 without conflicts.

Kind: fix
What: the rejection sampler's draft-token gather kernel no longer recompiles per batch size (num_logits not
  specialized)
Upstream: vllm #66026 (open)
Cut-against: 0.33.0
Retires-when: the pin that carries #66026

